web/static/codex/welcome.md:9: forbidden player time: It explains the world's day length, movement, your Host's reserves, food, the gods, travelling orders and the path to bronze. Its amounts come from the rules when that Host begins. Your Host lives on subsistence before founding, so its reserves do not drain while you search for a home. When you begin again, your standing with the gods is kept.
web/static/codex/welcome.md:15: forbidden player time: **Days belong to the world.** The world advances one day at a time, and every duration the game shows you counts in days. See [[time]].
web/static/codex/upkeep.md:1: forbidden player time: Soldiers eat **food: grain, fish, oil and wine**, shared in proportion to the stocks available. Professionals also need **silver pay** every day.
web/static/codex/upkeep.md:5: forbidden player time: **Field pay starts at 1 silver per 100 people per day**, the wage of a worker. Spearmen draw **0 silver**. A full hundred-man elite infantry cohort draws **1.5 silver in the field and 0.75 at home**; a full chariot cohort draws **4 in the field and 2 at home**. Remaining men determine the bill, so a half-strength cohort draws half the pay.
web/static/codex/upkeep.md:13: forbidden player time: An empty ship **Laid up** in its own Harbour costs **0 silver and 0 ship food**. Its crew has returned to the city’s population and eats as civilians. **Fit out** drafts that crew back immediately; home-rate ship food and, for a War Galley, half field pay resume while the ship spends three days preparing. See [[sea]].
web/static/codex/upkeep.md:17: forbidden player time: Soldiers use the same four [[food|food goods]] as the people. Livestock remains emergency food for civilians, not an army ration. A full spearman cohort uses **0.5 food per day in garrison**, or **1 food in the field**. A unit in garrison eats from its city. **A unit in the field eats double**, and there is nothing to forage — so a long march, a siege line or a posted watch is a real cost ([[stances]]). 
web/static/codex/upkeep.md:19: forbidden player time: **Rowers eat like soldiers.** A ship feeds its actual surviving crew: each rower uses the same food as a spearman. A full Galley (20 rowers) uses **0.10 food per day in port** or **0.20 on a mission**; a War Galley (50) uses **0.25 / 0.50**, and an Emporos (10) **0.05 / 0.10**. Fewer surviving rowers eat less. Garrisoned ships, repairs and fitting out use the port ration. Marching, posted and freighting ships use the field ration, including a freight route's wait between legs. A laid-up hull has no ship ration because its crew has returned to the city. Ship pay follows the home and field rates above.
web/static/codex/upkeep.md:21: forbidden player time: A unit that runs out of food **starves**: it loses men each day, and can starve to nothing. A ship turns home when its remaining food is only enough for the way back, unless it can reach a city for provisions. Its owner hears the reason when it reaches home, not from far away; see [[ship-food-return]].
web/static/codex/units.md:22: forbidden player time: A cohort is drafted out of your city's population and stays out of it until disbanded. Soldiers still eat. **Spearmen, Galleys and Emporoi draw 0 silver per day**. Elite infantry, chariots and War Galleys draw pay in [[silver]]. Home service costs half field pay: a full elite cohort draws 0.75 / 1.5 silver per day at home / in the field, and a full chariot cohort 2 / 4. A full War Galley draws 0.25 / 0.5 ([[upkeep]]). An army bigger than your city can feed and pay is not a strong army; it is a famine with spears.
web/static/codex/units.md:28: forbidden player time: An empty ship in your own active Harbour can be **Laid up** from its Army card. Its crew returns to the port's population and can work there; the hull draws no food or silver and remains in port. The card says **Laid up** and offers **Fit out**. Fitting out takes the ship's full crew from that city's current population and prepares it for **three days**. It cannot sail or be cancelled during that time, and its crew draws home-rate food and, for a War Galley, half field pay from the start. If the city is annexed, its laid-up and fitting-out ships go with the city. Disbanding a laid-up ship removes the hull without returning the same crew twice; choose the particular hull to disband by its name. Keryx: `unit lay-up --unit ID` and `unit fit-out --unit ID`.
web/static/codex/units.md:32: forbidden player time: Every unit you own, with its state and current grain and silver upkeep per day in plain words. **March** is shown while it can move; a damaged land cohort garrisoned in its **original home city** can also **Reinforce** — another city you own does not qualify. A land unit on the march shows **Recall**. The card also shows stance and **Set**, battle retreat, **Redirect**, **Fetch by ship**, **Load** / **Unload** or **Repair**, whenever that unit can use them. Choose **Destination city** in a march or redirect form to target a known city quickly; map clicks remain available. Keryx accepts `--to "City name"` on march, post and redirect. See [[marching]], [[stances]] and [[sea]].
web/static/codex/transfers.md:3: forbidden player time: Open **Economy → Transfer**, choose *From*, *To*, the good and the quantity, and press **Send →**. Below, **Your cargo in transit** lists everything on the road — which city it left, which city it is bound for, and the day it arrives. A notification tells you when it is delivered.
web/static/codex/transfers.md:31: forbidden player time: same path**, rounded once to the nearest whole day, with at least one day per leg.
web/static/codex/transfers.md:37: forbidden player time: arrival day shown in your cargo list is the scheduled delivery; delays or
web/static/codex/trade.md:12: forbidden player time: Then **Dispatch →**. The offer walks with the [[messengers|messenger]], so it takes days to arrive.
web/static/codex/trade.md:18: forbidden player time: The runner who carried your offer waits at the other city only while the offer is open: once it is accepted, declined or cancelled, the runner turns for home that same day. **You learn the answer when the runner is home** — the news travels with the runner, never ahead of it. A declined offer's escrow travels with the runner too: your goods or silver are back in your stores the day the runner is home. If the runner is lost at sea on the way, the escrow comes back at once. If nobody answers, you are told when the offer expires.
web/static/codex/trade.md:42: forbidden player time: same path**, rounded once to the nearest whole day, with at least one day per leg.
