web/static/codex/time.md:1: forbidden player time: ## Days and the clock
web/static/codex/time.md:3: forbidden player time: The world advances one **day** at a time. Wherever the game tells you how long something takes — a march, a building, a letter — it counts in days, because that is the unit your decisions are made in.
web/static/codex/time.md:5: forbidden player time: How much real time a day takes is set per world. A test world can run faster than normal. The **date in the top bar** is the world's own calendar; the sun and moon beside it show the time of day. **When something happens** uses your clock and date: **today 18:34**, **tomorrow 09:10**, or **Thu 09:00**; further away, a date is shown. **How long something lasts** stays in days of the world: **food for 12 days**, or **a 30-day expedition**. Arrival estimates follow the world's current pace; they can change when the world pauses or its pace changes.
web/static/codex/temples.md:13: forbidden player time: Each placed temple worker earns **1.05 kharis per day** before the offering factor, scarcity and realm decay. A fully staffed level-1 temple earns **2.1** before these adjustments. Kult shows local staffing and the projected realm net. 
web/static/codex/temples.md:17: forbidden player time: Each temple has its own daily reference gift of **0.25 grain**, taken only from its own city's stock. When a city holds several temples, they are planned in god-ID order — Asterion, Potnia, the Sea Daimons of Stillness, Velchanos — each against what the earlier ones left in the store. A temple's workers earn kharis only on days that temple was fed. Kharis stays one pool for the whole realm. Fish, timber and silver can replace grain at the same divine value; a gift may combine goods. The amount of a substitute can change with the world's divine values. 
web/static/codex/temples.md:21: forbidden player time: **Kult → Temple offerings** and `keryx status` show one row per temple with the reference, equivalent alternatives, the stock it would find after earlier temples, and the planned next gift, with a ✓ or ✗. If the city cannot fund the whole gift, nothing is taken and that temple earns no offering gain for the day. Devotion and the daily decay still determine your net kharis change.
web/static/codex/storms.md:5: forbidden player time: Each day a storm takes **two steps**. In each step, one of its three hexes moves to a neighbouring sea hex, and the storm stays in one piece. So a storm crawls: slowly, and a little more each day in the direction it has been drifting, now and then turning. It never crosses land.
web/static/codex/storms.md:9: forbidden player time: A ship that **sails into a storm** takes two points of hull damage (of five) — once per storm, however many days it spends inside it; three storms sink a ship from full hull. At hull 0 she founders with everything aboard ([[sea]]). A ship beside a storm is safe. Ships follow the route they were given and cannot steer round a storm once they sail ([[sea]]), so look before you send them.
web/static/codex/storms.md:11: forbidden player time: Every sea hex a ship crosses is checked, even when it crosses several in one day. Two different storms crossed that day each strike; staying inside the same storm gives no extra blow. A storm can also drift onto a slow ship. [[rites|Favourable Passage]] still protects the ship on its outward leg.
web/static/codex/storms.md:19: forbidden player time: Once you have seen a storm, the map remembers it **where you last saw it**, as a faded cloud, and hovering says on which day. That is not where it is now. If you look at the place and it is gone, it has moved on and the memory is dropped.
web/static/codex/silver.md:3: forbidden player time: Professionals draw pay every day. A Wanax who runs dry risks their desertion — see [[upkeep]].
web/static/codex/sight.md:32: forbidden player time: You cannot **march** an army into land none of your people has ever seen — but you can **explore** it. Right-click a hex, seen or unseen, land or sea, and choose **Explore**: the unit sets out as an **expedition** to the land within 5 hexes of that place, for as many days as you give it (4 to 365; 10 if you do not say).
web/static/codex/sight.md:35: forbidden player time: - **The order reserves time for the journey home.** It turns back no later than halfway through its days, so it is home by the end of them. It turns sooner if nothing in the area is left unseen, or if what is left cannot be reached. You are told when it turns, and why. If its home is lost during the expedition, it seeks another reachable city of yours; its mission line then shows the actual return day. Without a reachable home it stops where it is.
web/static/codex/sight.md:39: forbidden player time: - Out in the field it eats double, from home, like any unit away from its city ([[upkeep]]). A ship takes on food for every day of the expedition before it sails ([[sea]]).
web/static/codex/ship-food-return.md:1: forbidden player time: A ship at sea **turns home early** when its food is only enough for the voyage home, with one day in reserve. It uses its actual crew and any carried soldiers to work out the ration. A ship heading to a city it can still reach with its food may continue there for provisions instead.
web/static/codex/sea.md:13: forbidden player time: **Lay up** an idle, empty ship in your own active Harbour from War → Army when you do not need it. The crew joins the port's population, so the ship costs no food or silver and cannot sail, carry goods or take a passenger. **Fit out** drafts its crew again and takes three days before the ship is ready. The crew draws home-rate food and, for War Galleys, half field pay while fitting out. A laid-up hull follows its port if the city is annexed. See [[units]] and [[upkeep]].
web/static/codex/sea.md:17: forbidden player time: - **A ship is given its whole mission in port, then sails alone.** From **War → Army**, **Load** a land unit onto a ship standing with it, then send it on a **mission**: **Land** puts the cargo ashore on open ground and sails home again on its own — select "…and found a colony" to settle right there, no further order needed. The port pays the colonists' silver the moment the ship sails, as it would for a column on foot ([[colonies]]); if the colony cannot be founded, or you unload them at home, the silver comes back with them. **Patrol** and **Explore** return home by themselves. For **Explore**, choose an area and duration in days: the port loads provisions for the whole expedition, and the ship turns home by half the duration ([[sight]]). **Passage** is a fourth mission, empty of cargo, given to carry a [[messengers|letter]] or [[runners|order]] — see "Arrange passage" below.
web/static/codex/sea.md:20: forbidden player time: - **Crew pay.** Each surviving rower draws the worker’s wage: 1 silver per hundred rowers per day in the field, half at home. Full crews cost Galley **0.2 / 0.1**, War Galley **0.5 / 0.25**, Emporos **0.1 / 0.05** silver per day in the field / at home. Repairs and fitting out count as home; marching, posted and freight missions count as field service, including waits between freight legs. A laid-up ship draws no pay. Carried soldiers draw their own field pay ([[upkeep]]).
web/static/codex/sea.md:21: forbidden player time: - **Supplies.** Rowers eat the same per-person ration as spearmen, doubled while marching, posted or freighting ([[upkeep]]). The actual surviving crew determines the ration, and any carried soldiers need their own field rations too. A crew eats grain, fish, oil and wine, with the same proportional sharing as the city. The port loads food for the voyage from these four goods; livestock is not ship provision. War → Army shows food for N days and the goods aboard, including ships carrying goods on a freight route and waiting between its legs. Keryx `unit list` shows the same supply. Unused provisions come home as the goods that were loaded, so fish does not turn into grain. A ship turns home before its food runs too low for the voyage back, unless it can reach a city for provisions. You hear why when it reaches home; see [[ship-food-return]]. Ports feed visiting ships as guests; see [[xenia]]. At sea it eats only what was loaded aboard, never from a city's granary. The food gauge counts whole days; "less than a day" means the last ration is being eaten, "out of food" means nothing is left.
web/static/codex/sea.md:25: forbidden player time: The open sea is never quite safe, but **you can see its storms** — see [[storms]]. A storm is three sea hexes of dark cloud that drift slowly across the Thalassa; a ship that crosses any of them takes the damage, even between the day's start and end, whatever the ship is doing — a fleet, a ship carrying an army, an expedition, a galley carrying a trade, a transfer or a gift. A storm can also drift onto a slow ship. Whose ship it is and why it sails never change that. Rivers and land have no storms. 
web/static/codex/sea.md:27: forbidden player time: - **Each storm costs the ship two points of hull** (of five), once, however many days it spends inside that storm. Three storms sink a ship that began with full hull. You are told where it struck and how much hull is left.
web/static/codex/sea.md:88: forbidden player time: - **The ship waits, but not forever.** You choose how many days it holds off the shore. If the unit reaches the shore in time, it boards at once and the ship sails home with it. If the wait runs out first, the ship sails home empty — you are told either way.
web/static/codex/sea.md:96: forbidden player time: day**. Rivers allow **2 hexes per day**; a ford takes **1 day** for a ship.
web/static/codex/runners.md:5: forbidden player time: While the Runner is still on the road, the order shows under the unit in **War → Army** and in `keryx army`, with the day he is expected — it disappears once he has delivered it.
web/static/codex/runners.md:31: forbidden player time: **Diplomacy → Runners** (or `keryx runners`) shows every runner of yours that is still out, in groups: **waiting for a ship**, **on the way**, **aboard a ship**, **waiting for an answer**, and **on the way home**. A waiting runner says where it stands, since when, and why — and offers **Arrange passage** (one of your own free ships) or **Call back** (only when it waits in your own port). A runner **waiting for an answer** has reached the other Wanax's city and stands there until they answer — the tab says since when, and when it heads home if nobody does. An order's runner names the order it carries. Times are the clock time something happens — "today 18:34" — never a day count.
web/static/codex/runner-lost-at-sea.md:13: forbidden player time: An older sealed record may have no preserved departure day; it says "Sent on an unknown day" rather than guessing.
web/static/codex/routes.md:28: forbidden player time: same path**, rounded once to the nearest whole day, with at least one day per leg.
web/static/codex/routes.md:34: forbidden player time: arrival day shown in your cargo list is the scheduled delivery; delays or
web/static/codex/roads.md:10: forbidden player time: | Foot soldiers | 0.75 day |
web/static/codex/roads.md:11: forbidden player time: | Runners and messengers | 0.35 day |
web/static/codex/roads.md:12: forbidden player time: | Caravans | 0.6 day |
web/static/codex/roads.md:13: forbidden player time: | War chariots | 0.4 day |
web/static/codex/roads.md:16: forbidden player time: (Off the road a foot soldier needs 1 day on plains, 2 on hills and 3 in semi-desert, so a road helps most where the ground is hardest.)
web/static/codex/roads.md:33: forbidden player time: Three plains edges cost 24 stone. A hundred men build a plains edge in **1 day** and a hill edge in **2**; fewer men take longer. When the edge is finished you get a **RoadEdgeBuilt** notice.
web/static/codex/rites.md:13: forbidden player time: An answered prayer starts that god's waiting time across all your cities. **Kult** and `keryx rite --list` show the remaining wait, an active gift or a gift awaiting departure. Missing temples and insufficient kharis are named as separate requirements. Potnia's two prayers share her 24-day wait. An active gift cannot be renewed before it ends. A gift awaiting departure cannot be renewed before it is used.
web/static/codex/rites.md:24: forbidden player time: `keryx status` and `keryx city`, with its yield bonus, affected goods and days left.
web/static/codex/rites.md:29: forbidden player time: - **Potnia's Vision** — offer 10 grain + 3 silver. Potnia marks one source of copper, tin, silver or incense you neither see nor remember, and unknown ground within two hexes, as grey memory. It creates no living eyes and shows no moving activity. Potnia waits 24 days after answering.
web/static/codex/rites.md:30: forbidden player time: - **Mistress of the Walls** — offer 8 grain + 4 timber. Potnia strengthens your own garrison defending this city by a quarter for 6 days. It helps neither an attacking army nor soldiers away from that city. It shares Vision's wait.
web/static/codex/rites.md:31: forbidden player time: - **Green Shoots** — offer 15 grain + 2 silver. Velchanos raises grain, oil and wine yield by a quarter for 25 days. Fish and the herd are unchanged. He waits 25 days after answering.
web/static/codex/rites.md:33: forbidden player time: - **The Thread Shows a Path** — offer 4 timber + 3 silver to Asterion. The next land caravan or Runner leaving this city takes a quarter less time on its outward leg. A land caravan can be intercepted only within one hex of an enemy army, instead of two. Runners remain sacred. Asterion waits 12 days after answering.
web/static/codex/rites.md:34: forbidden player time: - **Favourable Passage** — offer 6 fish + 3 silver to The Sea Daimons of Stillness. The next ship leaving this city takes a quarter less time and suffers no storm damage on its outward leg. A Runner aboard travels at that ship's actual speed. The Sea Daimons wait 12 days after answering.
web/static/codex/rites.md:38: forbidden player time: A departure gift stays in the city where you offered until an eligible journey physically leaves. It is used once. It cannot follow the city to a new owner, change a journey already underway, or bless a return leg. **Awaiting next departure** appears beside the prayer while the gift is unused; the god's waiting time is shown separately. Other ongoing gifts show their remaining days.
web/static/codex/rites.md:40: forbidden player time: In Keryx, `rite --list` shows your prayers, their effects, offerings, active days, pending departure gifts and waiting time. `rite --prayer <id>` chooses one. Omitting the ID uses the prayer marked as the culture's default — Mistress of the Walls for Minoans.
web/static/codex/report.md:9: forbidden player time: Write what happened and press **Send**. Your position on the map, the current day and what you were looking at are attached automatically, so you do not need to describe them.
web/static/codex/occupation.md:11: forbidden player time:   on the map for the rest of that day, for anyone who has eyes on it, and stands as a ruin after;
web/static/codex/messengers.md:15: forbidden player time: A letter to a distant city takes days, and the reply takes as long again. You are told when your messenger arrives, and again when he is home with the answer. Plan diplomacy the way you plan a march.
web/static/codex/messengers.md:19: forbidden player time: A newly delivered plain letter can be answered on its arrival day and the following day. The bearer leaves at the start of the next day. **Letters** shows the departure time with the local departure time. Once the return-post window closes, you cannot reply through that bearer, even while his departure is still being processed. Reading does not extend his stay. A previously issued longer stay keeps its saved deadline. Trade offers keep their own answer deadline.
web/static/codex/marching.md:45: forbidden player time: Choose **Explore** in the map menu; the default expedition duration is ready to use. Open **More** to change the duration in **days**. The chosen hex is the centre of an area, even in fog. The order summary shows its extent and duration before sending; **War → Army → March** offers the same controls.
web/static/codex/marching.md:47: forbidden player time: It travels first to the exact hex you chose, even if that hex is already seen, then chooses nearby unexplored ground on its own. It turns home by half the duration. It can turn earlier if the area is already explored or nothing further is reachable. **War → Army** and `keryx unit list` show the area, duration, latest turn and home day, and why it has turned. A Runner-delivered order begins when received, so those days become known when it starts. On return, Dispatches carries the report ([[sight]]).
web/static/codex/marching.md:55: forbidden player time: For foot soldiers, each plain, river valley or delta takes **1 day**; olive groves
web/static/codex/marching.md:56: forbidden player time: and maquis **1.5 days**; hills and cedar forest **2 days**; semi-desert **3 days**.
web/static/codex/marching.md:57: forbidden player time: A ford takes **2.5 days** on foot. Mountains cannot be crossed.
web/static/codex/marching.md:58: forbidden player time: War chariots are fast on the flat and slow everywhere else: **0.67 days** on a
web/static/codex/marching.md:59: forbidden player time: plain, river valley or delta, **2 days** in olive groves and maquis, and
web/static/codex/marching.md:60: forbidden player time: **3 days** in hills, cedar forest and semi-desert. A ford takes them 2.5 days
web/static/codex/marching.md:64: forbidden player time: A fully crewed Galley sails **3 coastal hexes per day** or **5 deep-sea hexes per
web/static/codex/marching.md:65: forbidden player time: day**, **2 river hexes per day**, and takes **1 day** through a ford. A War Galley
web/static/codex/marching.md:71: forbidden player time: These are base costs before rounding the whole journey to at least one day.
web/static/codex/marching.md:73: forbidden player time: its recorded costs and arrival day when the movement rules change. A finished
web/static/codex/kharis.md:5: forbidden player time: | **Favorable** | The gods may bless you: a blessed harvest (a quarter more food for 25 days, fish excepted) or unexpected recruits. |
web/static/codex/kharis.md:7: forbidden player time: | **Suspicious** (5 to below 30) | Below 15, a 3% chance of punishment each day, with or without a temple; recent punishment gives seven game days of protection. |
web/static/codex/kharis.md:18: forbidden player time: For **three cities with 2,500 residents altogether**, upkeep is **0.1 + 0.4 + 0.25 = 0.75 kharis per day**. A realm without a city has the base cost only.
web/static/codex/kharis.md:20: forbidden player time: **Kult → Upkeep of the cult** and `keryx status` show the base, cities and residents separately, alongside projected temple production and net. One more fed temple worker gives **at least +1.05 kharis per day**, and more when the cult’s reference offering of grain is scarce in the world. This describes the extra worker's yield: employing one also requires a free citizen, an available temple workplace and that temple's daily offering. A temple earns favour; it does not remove its city's cost.
web/static/codex/kharis.md:22: forbidden player time: Below **15 Kharis**, there is a 3% chance of punishment each day **with or without a temple**. An actual punishment gives **seven game days of protection**. A game day follows this world's pace; Kult and `keryx status` show the protection's end in your local clock. Nothing taken means no new protection. See [[divine-wrath]]. A punishment chooses a random eligible city of yours with something it can take; it need not strike the capital.
web/static/codex/kharis.md:26: forbidden player time: Three things must be true, every day:
web/static/codex/kharis.md:29: forbidden player time: 2. **People serve in it** — placed temple workers, 2 workplaces per level in each temple. Each earns 1.05 kharis per day before the offering factor, scarcity and decay. See [[temples]].
web/static/codex/kharis.md:32: forbidden player time: In a small realm, a fully staffed, fed level-1 temple can earn more favour than the realm loses each day. Compare the projected net; a larger realm may need more temples or higher levels. Larger temples can employ more devotion and raise kharis faster when staffed and fed.
web/static/codex/kharis.md:36: forbidden player time: Kharis decays every day regardless. Additional cities and residents increase cult upkeep even when every city has a temple ([[colonies]]).
web/static/codex/kharis.md:41: forbidden player time: `keryx status` and `keryx city`, with its yield bonus, affected goods and days left.
web/static/codex/kharis.md:46: forbidden player time: A notification marks each change between **Favorable**, **Indifferent**, **Suspicious** and **Wrathful**, with the new Kharis value and recorded day. Check **Kult** for decay and temple support; staff and feed your [[temples]].
web/static/codex/keryx.md:35: forbidden player time: Orders given through Keryx obey the same rules as in the browser: they travel by [[runners|Runner]] and take days ([[time]]).
web/static/codex/keryx.md:41: forbidden player time: Use a city name or province ID either positionally or with `--province` in `city`, `placements`, `status` and `build`. For placement: `keryx place Knossos fish 13 -1` or `keryx place --province Knossos fish 13 -1`. Put flags before the good when using a negative delta. If no citizens work that good on the chosen hex, the result lists where they do work it. `founding settle --yes` skips the question. Travel estimates use days; if an arrival cannot be calculated it is reported as unavailable.
web/static/codex/horde.md:9: forbidden player time: - **The Host itself eats nothing.** A people on the move carries its own bread. The grain in the store is a dowry that goes into your city's stores the day you found.
web/static/codex/harbour.md:10: forbidden player time: Each deep-water fisher catches **1 fish per day**, at every harbour level. The harbour has no fishing multiplier and adds no coastal places. Coastal water always has two places producing 1 fish per fisher per day; a bay has two places producing 2 each. See [[fishing]] for the bay rule. Rivers and fords receive no harbour bonus.
web/static/codex/growth.md:1: forbidden player time: A city grows when it has a **surplus of food** — when it produces more food each day than its people eat. Growing costs no food: the surplus stays in the same city grain, fish, oil and wine stocks that feed your people.
web/static/codex/growth.md:5: forbidden player time: - **Variety.** Any one of the four food goods feeds people. Growth gains 10% per extra kind that forms a real share of the food eaten that day, up to the existing variety ceiling: two substantial kinds give a 1.1 multiplier and four give 1.3. Livestock counts only if eaten as a real share of emergency food. A good merely sitting in store, or eaten only in crumbs, gives no variety bonus.
web/static/codex/growth.md:8: forbidden player time: In short: a city grows only while it makes more food than it eats; it holds steady when it does not; and it shrinks when it goes hungry. The city screen and `keryx status` say which of the three it is, and the food net behind it, for example "not growing: food net -1.7 per day (needs more than 0)".
web/static/codex/goods.md:16: forbidden player time: - **Goods** — stock, production and use of each good, city by city. The settlements table compares people, food produced minus food eaten, food days and shortage status; select a city to close Economy and open its City drawer. Goods piling up with nothing using them are marked — see [[surplus]].
web/static/codex/goods.md:26: forbidden player time: Production per day is output before meals and other uses. Food net combines grain, fish, oil and wine production minus population demand. The city has one stock of each good; food coverage counts these four goods together. Shortfall waiting estimates assume current production continues, before other use.
web/static/codex/goods.md:33: forbidden player time: Each good has its own storage capacity in each city. A city stores **2 000 of each food** — grain, fish, oil and wine — and a [[buildings|Granary]] raises that to 4 000, 10 000 and 20 000. The herd has its own pens. A city that already held more food when this ceiling came in keeps it and can eat it, but it never fills back above the ceiling. **StorageCapacityWarning** is an advance dispatch when the server forecasts a stock nearing its cap. Keryx status and the City drawer show the good, stored amount, capacity and **full in N days**. The estimate uses the current net rate and deliveries on the way; it is a forecast, so changing production or orders can change it.
web/static/codex/goods.md:39: forbidden player time: A **StorageOverflow** notification means goods have already been lost because storage was full. It records the city, good, quantity lost and the source, such as production, a delivery, returned provisions or a refund. It does not mean the entire shipment was lost: only the reported excess failed to fit. Production into a store that stays full is lost every day; you are told when it starts, not every day it goes on. If the forecast clears and the city later approaches capacity again, a new warning can be sent.
web/static/codex/glossary.md:27: forbidden player time: **Day** — the world’s unit of time; the calendar names its date. See [[time]].
web/static/codex/getting-in.md:10: forbidden player time: A new world stands still until enough Wanaxes have entered it. Until then no day passes: nothing is produced, eaten or delivered — and no orders are taken. You can look around, read this Codex and file reports, but every order is refused with *"The world has not begun — 1 of 4 Wanaxes have arrived"* (the numbers are the world's own) until the last of them enters. This is deliberate, so that whoever arrives first does not grow on everyone else's waiting time.
web/static/codex/founding.md:11: forbidden player time: - **It does not feed itself** — you are told how many days your carried grain buys, and whether a Farm would fix it. If it says a farm will not help, there is no farmable ground in reach at all.
web/static/codex/founding.md:15: forbidden player time: In **Details**, below the grain line, look for **deposits**: copper, tin, silver, cedar. A site with metal under it is worth walking extra days for. A site with none is not a mistake — you can buy metal ([[trade]]) — but you will be buying it from someone who walked those days. Read [[bronze]] before you decide.
web/static/codex/founding.md:40: forbidden player time: Place citizens on olive groves, maquis, hills or river valleys for timber, or on cedar forest for cedar to cover timber costs, and on stone-bearing ground for stone; idle citizens produce nothing. Maquis, hills and river valleys each have one timber place before a Lumbermill is built, yielding 0.33 timber per worker per day ([[catchment]]). The forecast says when Demeter’s farm is already included; another farm is not an extra founding gift.
web/static/codex/founding.md:42: forbidden player time: On grain-bearing hills, a Farm at level 1 makes 0.85 grain per worker per day, with room for five workers. Plain hills give 0.5 per worker. With another grain-bearing hill in reach, six food workers can feed a new city of 1,000 people; leave the rest for timber and stone. Check the forecast for your actual site.
web/static/codex/food.md:1: forbidden player time: Your people eat **grain, fish, oil and wine** every day. One unit of each supplies one unit of food. The meal is shared in proportion to what is available, including the day’s production: a city with 100 grain and 900 fish takes one tenth of its meal as grain and nine tenths as fish. No food good is used up first. If these four cannot feed the people, livestock remains their emergency food: each animal supplies 3 food. Armies, ships and caravans use the four food goods, not livestock.
web/static/codex/food.md:5: forbidden player time: If the available food and emergency livestock run out and the day's need is still unmet:
web/static/codex/food.md:7: forbidden player time: - the settlement **loses part of its population that day**, and keeps losing while the shortfall lasts;
web/static/codex/food.md:11: forbidden player time: You get an urgent **CivilianStarvationStarted** notification when hunger is first observed. It records the day, city and how much of that day's meal was missing, in food. The latest start stays above ordinary history in Notifications until that city's food supply has recovered steadily. You get **CivilianStarvationEnded** after a run of fully fed days; the old start then returns to ordinary history. Hunger returning during that recovery window belongs to the same warning. A new spell after recovery starts a new pair of notices, rather than repeating the warning every day.
web/static/codex/food.md:19: forbidden player time: The herd breeds itself — no herder is needed. Every ten animals give one calf every **5 days** when the city has grain to feed it (**1 grain per calf**), and every **20 days** on pasture alone. If the grain does not reach every calf, the fed ones come on the 5-day beat and the rest wait for the 20-day beat. A city can only keep so many animals: the **herd cap** is 50, and every level of a **Pen** ([[buildings]]) adds 100. **Status** and **City → Food** show it as *Herd 50 / 50*, with the next calves ("4 calves in 3 days (4 grain)"). Each animal is worth **3 food**. Animals **above** the cap are eaten **first** at the day's meal — fresh meat does not keep — and the four goods share whatever hunger is left. If animals are still above the cap after the meal, the pen cannot hold them and they are **lost**. A bigger Pen keeps more of them. Animals under the cap stay in the herd and are eaten only if the people would otherwise starve.
web/static/codex/food.md:29: forbidden player time: Your city has food stocks: the grain, fish, oil and wine in its [[goods|stores]]. **Food for N days** shows those stocks at current consumption, without counting future production. **City → More → Food details** shows production, the last meal by good and army food separately. Different goods forming a real share of the meal improve [[growth|population growth]]; merely storing another food does not. Stored food buys time during a shortfall. A city under [[sieges|siege]] starves because its fields are cut off, not because it produces too little.
web/static/codex/fishing.md:3: forbidden player time: | Water | Fishing places | Fish per fisher per day |
web/static/codex/fishing.md:13: forbidden player time: A harbour creates places, not workers. Put citizens on the deep-water hexes to use them. Higher harbour levels open more places there, while each fisher still catches 1 fish per day.
web/static/codex/fishing.md:15: forbidden player time: **A storm stops the fishing.** While a [[storms|storm]] stands on a fishing hex, the citizens placed there catch nothing. They fish again the day it has moved on. **City** shows *Storm over N fishing grounds*, and `keryx city` and `keryx status` print the same line.
web/static/codex/divine-wrath.md:1: forbidden player time: The gods may punish a realm **below 15 kharis**, with or without a [[temples|temple]]. Each day brings a **3% chance**. At exactly 15 there is no risk. A punishment chooses one of your active cities that has something it can take.
web/static/codex/divine-wrath.md:5: forbidden player time: When your standing crosses **30 downward** and the projected net is negative, a warning tells you your kharis, net per day and how many days it would take to reach 15 at that rate. It is sent once per downward crossing. A positive or zero net gives no warning. Further decline below 30 does not repeat it; recovery above 30 allows a new downward crossing to warn again.
web/static/codex/coming-back.md:17: forbidden player time: 1. **Food** — a [[food|shortfall]] kills people every day it lasts.
web/static/codex/coming-back.md:22: forbidden player time: A [[kharis|Kharis]] notice marks each change between Favorable, Indifferent, Suspicious and Wrathful. Check **Kult** for Kharis and the upkeep of the cult. It fades from the start; every active city and its residents count toward the cost. A warning marks a fall below 30 with negative net; below 15 punishment can fall, with or without a temple. An actual punishment protects you for seven days; Kult shows when this protection ends. Staff and feed temples, then check the projected net.
web/static/codex/city.md:11: forbidden player time: - **Food** — how many days the city’s current grain, fish, oil and wine stocks last at current consumption, whether stocks are growing, and whether the fields can feed everyone. See [[food]].
web/static/codex/city.md:13: forbidden player time: Open **More** for people in service and the labour pool, **Devotion** ([[temples]]), **Food details** ([[food]]), **Last day**, and **Daily history → Show recent days**. These details and controls remain available while the main view stays short.
web/static/codex/catchment.md:5: forbidden player time: **Maquis, hills and river valleys also give timber:** each hex has room for one timber worker, producing **0.33 timber per day**. A [[buildings|Lumbermill]] adds four places, making room for five timber workers, and multiplies each worker's output by ×1.7 at level 1, ×2.4 at level 2 or ×3.1 at level 3. Olive groves remain a richer source of timber; cedar forest produces cedar, which can cover timber costs.
web/static/codex/buildings.md:32: forbidden player time: Every other building, including **Harbour**, stands in the city itself. Harbour is the one exception worth naming: though it stands in the city (and needs the city's own hex to border the sea), its fishing effect reaches every deep-sea hex in your catchment at once. Harbour levels 1, 2 and 3 open 1, 2 and 4 places per deep-water hex; each fisher catches 1 fish per day. It gives no fishing multiplier or extra coastal places. See [[fishing]] and [[harbour]].
web/static/codex/battle.md:5: forbidden player time: A battle is not one roll. It runs over several days, and it always resolves the same way from the moment it begins. What decides it:
web/static/codex/battle.md:9: forbidden player time: - **Walls**, if a city is involved. A wall absorbs a fixed amount of damage each day — a shield, not a sword. **storm** halves it, at a price.
web/static/codex/battle.md:18: forbidden player time: **Mistress of the Walls** ([[rites]]) strengthens only the casting Wanax's own garrison defending the blessed city, for 6 days. It gives no bonus to an attacker, a field army or a new owner of the city.
web/static/codex/account.md:1: forbidden player time: Click your Wanax name at the top left to open **Account**. Its realm summary has five rows for your selected world: your Wanax name, founding day, owned active cities, their total population, and your realm's Kharis.
web/static/codex/index.json:7: forbidden player time: Days and the clock
